In our order-bound view, the man’s first station is “eldest son,” charged to honor his forebears by sustaining the workshop that embodies the family’s public role. Shutting it breaks a five-generation chain and lets his parents’ life-work die in their sight, a grave breach of filial duty that unsettles the wider community’s expectations.  

Yet once he marries, he also acquires the station of “household head,” responsible for wife and children. Their schooling and his wife’s craft contribute to the family’s future prosperity, so uprooting them is no minor matter. Still, that duty is junior to the ancestral one that legitimizes the family’s standing. Personal self-definition (Option C) carries little weight; social order rests on accepted ranks, not private choice.

