In our rank-and-role order, the first question is framed by two strong, competing claims. The son’s inherited station and the father’s authority normally set his duty; without reliable continuity, the household and many dependents are imperiled. Yet ability and temperament can be viewed as “talents entrusted by Heaven,” and if they are stifled the family may gain only a half-hearted steward. Hence, although the father’s claim remains primary, the pull of the young man’s nature still counts, leaving us to weigh which obligation is heavier here rather than to deny either one.

For understanding the case, the primacy of positional duty is decisive, but two further considerations carry real weight: a badly suited heir may ruin a business, and every worker supported by the firm has a moral claim that cannot be shuffled off.

