In a rank-and-role society, the father stands as the immediate superior responsible for the moral conduct of the subordinate (his son). If disorder created by the son is left unaddressed, it reflects failure of paternal stewardship and weakens the chain of accountability that keeps the community coherent. Insisting on confession both restores damaged order (property and trust) and publicly demonstrates that the hierarchy can police itself. Guiding yet deferring to the son would cede authority and risk signaling that rank carries no real responsibility; leaving the choice entirely to the boy places individual preference above ordered duty.

For what matters, the primary concern is the relational obligation of the superior (parent) to form a trustworthy subordinate who, in turn, upholds communal stability. Repairing the neighbor’s loss is also important because social peace depends on restitution, but it is secondary to affirming the father-son duty. Individual self-development is least weighty because moral agency is expected to be cultivated through guidance, not solitary choice.

