In this role-based society, hierarchy inside the family is a real moral fact: the older brother’s standing safeguards not only his personal dignity but the family’s public harmony. Yet the officials who offered the new post presumably judged that her gifts serve the larger social order, and refusing could waste talent that the hierarchy above her wishes to employ. Therefore the sound path is one that lets authority benefit from her abilities while she actively upholds her brother’s face—public gestures of deference, invocation of his mentorship, and so on. Pure self-sacrifice (A) protects harmony but ignores the higher authority’s call; pure self-assertion (B) disrespects rank; a mediated acceptance (C) fits both obligations.

For understanding the case, awareness of rank and dignity (A) is foundational, but so is the art of advancing without shaming seniors (C). Condemning the family structure itself (B) misses the society’s moral frame.

