In this community, the elder’s role exists precisely to keep public order. If younger members openly dispute the ruling, they risk fraying the hierarchy that keeps everyone oriented to their duties. Still, harmony also depends on the ruled feeling heard; giving no room at all for dissent could quietly breed resentment. Thus, deference to rank should dominate, yet there is space—within respectful channels—for the younger members’ concerns to reach the elder or a higher authority.

When thinking it through, the key lens is the community’s faith that traditional authority embodies accumulated wisdom. Outsiders may see only an arbitrary decree, but locals see continuity and stability. Fairness matters, yet mainly insofar as it is pursued without threatening the structure that upholds the common good. The view that “no authority is beyond challenge” is attractive in the abstract but clashes with a system whose very legitimacy rests on recognized, largely unquestioned offices.

