Among people who prize ordered roles, the first question is whether the joking breaches any duty attached to the speaker’s station. No member of the mocked group is present, so the speech does not directly insult someone under the speaker’s care, nor does it defy a superior’s command. Still, needlessly ridiculing the weak displays a coarse disposition unfitting to one who should model propriety for peers and subordinates; it hints at disorderly character more than at concrete harm. Private settings do slightly lessen the gravity, yet they do not erase the obligation to speak with decorum.

For evaluation, the most telling issue is how such talk shapes habits that may spill into the wider hierarchy: if contempt toward the weak is cultivated in private, it can corrode public solidarity. Context always matters in a rank-based ethic, because words that are harmless banter among equals could be subversive if spoken where juniors listen. Actual impact on absent persons has some weight, but the character and situational fit of the speaker weigh more.

